		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Réponse à&#160;:  Utilisation /tmp sur une VM Linux à l'export d'un dump au format TAR]]></title>
		<link rel="alternate" href="http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=35010#p35010"/>
		<content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>Je ne crois pas que YON utilise /tmp pour son export. pg_dump le fait tout seul lors de l&#039;utilisation du format tar. Les explications de Tom Lane sur ce comportement :</p><div class="quotebox"><blockquote><div><p>Tar format is sufficiently weird that we have to dump data into a temp<br />file and then append it to the archive after we know its exact size.<br />This means double copying of data, as well as problems if your temp<br />directory is in an undersized filesystem.&#160; On the whole, custom format<br />is a lot better bet from a performance standpoint --- so we only<br />recommend tar format if you have a clear need to be able to disassemble<br />the archive with non-Postgres tools.</p></div></blockquote></div><p>Quant à la corriger ce comportement, testez avec la variable d&#039;environnementTMPDIR mais toujours d&#039;après Tom Lane :</p><div class="quotebox"><blockquote><div><p>The temp files are created with the standard library function tmpfile().<br />If you think that&#039;s behaving improperly, take it up with your OS vendor.<br />I suspect they&#039;ll tell you it would be a security risk for that function<br />to respond to environment variables.</p></div></blockquote></div><p>Bref, le mieux est certainement d&#039;oublier le format tar et de passer par le format custom (ou directory).</p>]]></content>
